  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MHPSS Technical Expert Poland United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees (UNHCR)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Assignment at the request of UNHCR for a Technical Expert to support MHPSS Working Group in Poland. The war in Ukraine has prompted an outflux of 2.5 million refugees in a few weeks. As the situation continues to unfold, an estimated four million people may flee Ukraine. Most refugees went to Poland, which is hosting 1.5 million refugees including an important number of third country nationals (TNCs). The MHPSS Technical Expert will contribute to mental health and psychosocial support coordination and capacity building by assisting the work of MHPSS technical working group&#x2019;s work and actively support MHPSS actors by building capacities, conduct a detailed mapping of MHPSS stakeholders and activities, sharing best practices, building capacities of MHPSS actors and partners, supporting the development of MHPSS documents and strengthening advocacy initiatives.Both aims of MHPSS22001 (Hungary) and MHPSS22003 (Poland) were to promote that Mental Health and Psychosocial Support (MHPSS) was well integrated within the Ukraine refugee response in Hungary and Poland. At the beginning of 2022, several thousand Ukrainian families have fled their residence towards the western border of the country that neighbors with Moldova, Romania, Hungary, Slovakia, Poland, and Belarus. Worth noting that most of the neighboring countries to Ukraine were not coping with an emergency in decades and hence felt ill-equipped to face the demands of a humanitarian situation, including their capacities to respond to psycho-social needs of migrants and refugees. For this this reason, the MHPSS Surge Mechanism from The Netherlands Enterprise Agency (RVO) has provided support by deploying two experts to Hungary and Poland. This is why it is good to report these projects to IATI.</narrative>
  </description>
